Jira-like Task Management System — Code Explanation
Let's understand the code what each class does, why it is needed, and how objects interact when the program runs.

The system has four main responsibilities:
1. Task management: Create Stories, Features, and Bugs.
2. Task relationships: Add subtasks to Stories.
3. Sprint management: Create sprints and add or remove tasks.
4. Reporting: Find delayed tasks, display sprint details, and list tasks assigned to a user.
1. Understand the overall flow JiraDemo

Runs the application and calls operations

TaskManager

Coordinates tasks, sprints, and reports

Task hierarchy

Task → Story, Feature, Bug


Sprint

Contains selected tasks


The TaskManager is the coordinator. It maintains a central collection of tasks and another collection of sprints.
2. Enums: TaskStatus and TaskType
Your code defines:
enum TaskStatus {
    TODO,
    IN_PROGRESS,
    IN_REVIEW,
    DONE
}

enum TaskType {
    STORY,
    FEATURE,
    BUG
}


An enum represents a fixed set of possible values.

For example, a task can have the status TODO, IN_PROGRESS, IN_REVIEW, or DONE.

Instead of writing:
task.changeStatus("INPROGRESS"); // Can contain typos


you write:
task.changeStatus(TaskStatus.IN_PROGRESS);


Java can check that the value belongs to the TaskStatus enum.

The TaskType enum identifies whether a task is a Story, Feature, or Bug. In this code, each subclass returns its type using getType().
3. The User class
class User {
    private final String id;
    private final String name;

    public User(String id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }
}


A User represents the person responsible for tasks.

For example:
User alice = new User("U1", "Alice");
User bob = new User("U2", "Bob");


Alice has ID U1, while Bob has ID U2.

The final fields mean their IDs and names cannot be reassigned after construction. Getter methods provide access to these values.

The toString() method returns a readable representation, such as Alice (U1).
4. The abstract Task class
This is the most important class because Story, Feature, and Bug share common properties.
abstract class Task {
    private final String id;
    private final String title;
    private final String description;

    private TaskStatus status;
    private User assignee;
    private final LocalDate dueDate;
}


What does each field represent?
Field	Meaning
id	Unique task identifier
title	Short task name
description	Details about the task
status	Current progress
assignee	User responsible for the task
dueDate	Deadline

The constructor initializes these fields:
public Task(String id, String title, String description,
            User assignee, LocalDate dueDate) {
    this.id = id;
    this.title = title;
    this.description = description;
    this.assignee = assignee;
    this.dueDate = dueDate;
    this.status = TaskStatus.TODO;
}


Notice that every new task starts with TODO.

LocalDate represents a date without a time, and LocalDate.now() gets the current date.
Why is Task abstract?
public abstract TaskType getType();


A generic task does not specify whether it is a Story, Feature, or Bug. The subclasses supply that information.

Because Task is abstract, you cannot instantiate it directly:
// Not allowed:
Task task = new Task(...);


Instead, you create a concrete task:
Task task = new Bug(...);


This is an example of abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism.
5. Changing status and assigning tasks
changeStatus()
public void changeStatus(TaskStatus status) {
    this.status = Objects.requireNonNull(status);
}


Suppose the task's current status is TODO.
manager.changeStatus("T1", TaskStatus.IN_PROGRESS);


The task's status becomes IN_PROGRESS.

Objects.requireNonNull(status) prevents the status from being set to null.
assignTo()
public void assignTo(User user) {
    this.assignee = Objects.requireNonNull(user);
}


This changes the person responsible for a task.

For example, assigning a task to Bob means its assignee field now references the Bob object.
How does the task detect delays?
public boolean isDelayed() {
    return dueDate != null
            && dueDate.isBefore(LocalDate.now())
            && status != TaskStatus.DONE;
}


A task is delayed only when all three conditions are true:
1. A due date exists.
2. The due date is before today.
3. The task is not completed.
For example, if today is October 26 and the due date is October 24, a task in IN_PROGRESS is delayed. If that task is DONE, it is not delayed.
printDetails()
This method prints the task's type, ID, title, status, assignee, and due date.
System.out.println(
    "[" + getType() + "] " + id
    + " | " + title
    + " | Status: " + status
    + " | Assignee: "
    + (assignee == null ? "Unassigned" : assignee.getName())
    + " | Due: " + dueDate
);


The ternary expression:
assignee == null ? "Unassigned" : assignee.getName()


means if there is no assignee, print Unassigned; otherwise, print the user's name.
6. Story, Feature, and Bug
All three classes extend Task:
class Story extends Task { ... }
class Feature extends Task { ... }
class Bug extends Task { ... }


This means they inherit the common fields and methods instead of duplicating them.
Why call super()?
For example, in Feature:
public Feature(String id, String title, String description,
               User assignee, LocalDate dueDate) {
    super(id, title, description, assignee, dueDate);
}


super(...) calls the constructor of the parent class, Task, to initialize the shared fields.

Each subclass implements its own task type:
@Override
public TaskType getType() {
    return TaskType.FEATURE;
}


So, when printDetails() calls getType(), Java uses the implementation belonging to the actual object.

That is runtime polymorphism.
Why can only a Story contain subtasks?
Your code defines:
private final List<Task> subtasks = new ArrayList<>();


inside Story, not inside Task.

This means only a Story has a collection for subtasks in this design. A Story can contain a Feature or Bug as a subtask, but the code rejects a nested Story.

The addSubtask() method performs checks:
if (task == this) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "A story cannot contain itself");
}

if (task instanceof Story) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "Nested stories are not supported");
}


- task == this checks whether the Story is being added as its own subtask.
- instanceof Story checks whether the subtask is itself a Story.
The duplicate check prevents the same task ID from being added twice to the same Story.

Finally:
subtasks.add(task);


adds the subtask.

The getter:
return Collections.unmodifiableList(subtasks);


allows other classes to read the subtasks without directly adding or removing elements through the returned list.
7. The Sprint class
A sprint represents a time-boxed period during which a team works on selected tasks.
private final String id;
private final String name;
private final LocalDate startDate;
private final LocalDate endDate;

private final Map<String, Task> tasks = new LinkedHashMap<>();


The sprint has an ID, name, start date, end date, and a collection of tasks.
Why use Map<String, Task>?
The key is the task ID, and the value is the corresponding task object.

For example:
Sprint S1 — Sprint 1
T1
Story: User Login

T2
Feature: Dark Mode

The map provides quick lookup by task ID and preserves insertion order.

Validating the sprint dates
if (endDate.isBefore(startDate)) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "Sprint end date cannot precede start date");
}


This prevents creating a sprint whose end date is earlier than its start date.
Adding a task
public void addTask(Task task) {
    if (tasks.containsKey(task.getId())) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "Task already belongs to this sprint");
    }

    tasks.put(task.getId(), task);
}


The code first checks whether that task ID is already in the sprint. If it isn't, the task is inserted into the map.
Removing a task
public void removeTask(String taskId) {
    if (tasks.remove(taskId) == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "Task not found in this sprint");
    }
}


Map.remove() removes the entry and returns its previous value. If no entry exists, it returns null, which causes the exception.

Removing a task from a sprint does not delete the task from the main task collection.
Printing sprint details
The method prints the sprint's name, dates, and all its tasks. If the sprint has no tasks, it prints No tasks.

Notice that it calls:
task.printDetails();


Because every task type inherits or overrides this method, it prints the appropriate details for each task.
8. The TaskManager class
This class coordinates most of the system's operations. It maintains the master collections of tasks and sprints.
private final Map<String, Task> tasks = new LinkedHashMap<>();
private final Map<String, Sprint> sprints = new LinkedHashMap<>();


Think of these as the system's in-memory storage:
- tasks stores all registered tasks.
- sprints stores all created sprints.
The final keyword means these map references cannot be reassigned. The contents of the maps can still change.
A. Registering tasks
private void registerTask(Task task) {
    if (tasks.containsKey(task.getId())) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "Duplicate task ID: " + task.getId());
    }

    tasks.put(task.getId(), task);
}


This is a common helper method used by:
createStory(story);
createFeature(feature);
createBug(bug);


All three methods call registerTask(), so the system uses the same logic to check IDs and store tasks.

For example:
manager.createStory(story);


stores the Story in the central tasks map using its ID as the key.
B. Adding a subtask to a Story
public void addSubtask(String storyId, String taskId) {
    Task parent = getTask(storyId);
    Task child = getTask(taskId);

    if (!(parent instanceof Story)) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "Parent task must be a Story");
    }

    // Additional validation...

    ((Story) parent).addSubtask(child);
}


Let's say you call:
manager.addSubtask("T1", "T4");


The process is:
1. Find the parent task T1.
2. Find the child task T4.
3. Check that T1 is a Story.
4. Check that the child isn't the parent itself.
5. Check that the child doesn't already belong to another Story.
6. Cast the parent to Story and add the child.
The cast:
((Story) parent).addSubtask(child);


is required because parent is declared as Task, but addSubtask() exists only in the Story class.
C. Finding a task by ID
public Task getTask(String taskId) {
    Task task = tasks.get(taskId);

    if (task == null) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "Task not found: " + taskId);
    }

    return task;
}


This method retrieves a task from the central map.

If T1 exists, it returns the corresponding task object. If the ID doesn't exist, it throws an exception instead of returning null.

Several other methods reuse getTask(), which avoids repeating lookup and validation logic.
D. Changing status
public void changeStatus(String taskId, TaskStatus status) {
    getTask(taskId).changeStatus(status);
}


This is a good example of delegation.

TaskManager finds the task, then asks the task itself to change its status.

For example:
manager.changeStatus("T1", TaskStatus.IN_PROGRESS);


The manager doesn't directly modify the private status field. The Task class controls that operation.
E. Assigning a task
public void assignTask(String taskId, User user) {
    getTask(taskId).assignTo(user);
}


This follows the same approach: find the task, then delegate assignment to it.
F. Creating a sprint
public void createSprint(Sprint sprint) {
    if (sprints.containsKey(sprint.getId())) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
            "Duplicate sprint ID: " + sprint.getId());
    }

    sprints.put(sprint.getId(), sprint);
}


This validates that the sprint ID is unique and then registers the sprint.
G. Adding a task to a sprint
public void addTaskToSprint(String sprintId, String taskId) {
    Sprint sprint = getSprint(sprintId);
    Task task = getTask(taskId);

    for (Sprint existing : sprints.values()) {
        if (existing.containsTask(taskId)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Task already belongs to a sprint");
        }
    }

    sprint.addTask(task);
}


This method performs three important operations:
1. Find the requested sprint.
2. Find the requested task.
3. Check every sprint to ensure that the task isn't already assigned to another sprint.
If validation succeeds, it delegates the actual insertion to Sprint.addTask().

The loop is necessary because this implementation allows each task to belong to at most one sprint.
H. Removing a task from a sprint
public void removeTaskFromSprint(
        String sprintId, String taskId) {
    getSprint(sprintId).removeTask(taskId);
}


This retrieves the sprint and delegates removal to it.

The task remains in the master tasks map, so you can still change its status, assign it to a user, or include it in reports.
I. Printing delayed tasks
public void printDelayedTasks() {
    for (Task task : tasks.values()) {
        if (task.isDelayed()) {
            task.printDetails();
        }
    }
}


The manager checks every registered task and calls isDelayed().

It prints only the tasks whose due dates have passed and whose status is not DONE.
J. Printing tasks assigned to a user
public void printTasksAssignedTo(User user) {
    for (Task task : tasks.values()) {
        if (task.getAssignee() != null
                && task.getAssignee().getId().equals(user.getId())) {
            task.printDetails();
        }
    }
}


The manager compares user IDs rather than object references.

That matters because two different User objects could represent the same user ID. Comparing IDs allows the system to match them correctly.
9. Understand the main() method
The main() method demonstrates how a client uses the system.
Step 1: Create the manager and users
TaskManager manager = new TaskManager();

User alice = new User("U1", "Alice");
User bob = new User("U2", "Bob");


The manager is responsible for managing tasks and sprints. Alice and Bob are potential assignees.
Step 2: Create different task types
Story story = new Story(
    "T1", "User Login", "Implement login",
    alice, LocalDate.now().minusDays(2));


This creates a Story assigned to Alice with a due date two days ago.

The other statements create a Feature, a Bug, and another Feature that will be used as a subtask.
Step 3: Register all tasks
manager.createStory(story);
manager.createFeature(feature);
manager.createBug(bug);
manager.createFeature(subtask);


All four tasks are now available through the manager's master task map.

Important: Creating a task object and registering it are two separate steps in this design.
Step 4: Add a subtask
manager.addSubtask("T1", "T4");


The task T4 becomes a subtask of Story T1.
Step 5: Change statuses
manager.changeStatus("T1", TaskStatus.IN_PROGRESS);
manager.changeStatus("T3", TaskStatus.DONE);


Story T1 is now in progress, while Bug T3 is complete.

Since the Bug is marked DONE, it won't be reported as delayed, even though its due date is in the past.
Step 6: Create a sprint
Sprint sprint = new Sprint(
    "S1", "Sprint 1",
    LocalDate.now(),
    LocalDate.now().plusDays(14));

manager.createSprint(sprint);


This creates a sprint beginning today and ending 14 days later.
Step 7: Add and remove tasks
manager.addTaskToSprint("S1", "T1");
manager.addTaskToSprint("S1", "T2");
manager.addTaskToSprint("S1", "T3");

manager.removeTaskFromSprint("S1", "T3");


The sprint initially contains T1, T2, and T3. The last statement removes T3 from the sprint, but it doesn't delete it from the system.
Step 8: Generate reports
manager.printSprintDetails("S1");
manager.printDelayedTasks();
manager.printTasksAssignedTo(alice);


These statements print:
- The sprint's details and remaining tasks.
- All delayed tasks.
- All tasks assigned to Alice.
With the dates in this example, T1 is delayed because its due date is two days ago and it is still IN_PROGRESS. T3 is not delayed because its status is DONE. The subtask T4 is not delayed because its due date is in the future.
10. Design patterns and OOP concepts
Concept	Where used	Why
Abstraction	Task	Defines shared task behavior
Inheritance	Story, Feature, Bug	Reuses common fields and methods
Polymorphism	getType(), printDetails()	Allows different task types to behave appropriately
Encapsulation	Private fields and public methods	Controls access to internal state
Composition	Story contains subtasks	Models the parent-child relationship
Enum	TaskStatus, TaskType	Restricts values to defined choices
In-memory repository-like storage	Maps inside TaskManager	Provides centralized lookup and storage

The maps inside TaskManager act as simple in-memory storage, but they are not a full implementation of the Repository pattern because the persistence responsibility hasn't been separated into dedicated repository interfaces.
